(*
Issue #300's reopening comment: the reverted PR #553 (issues #550/#551's
first, abandoned fix attempt) added a term-count ceiling to
parseSimpleExpr/parseTerm's addop/mulop folding loops, reusing
Parser::MaxExprDepth = 500 -- a constant tuned for genuine recursive
'('-nesting depth (bounding parseFactor's OWN activation count), an
unrelated resource from an iteratively-folded flat chain, which never
grows the real C++ call stack per term the way '('-nesting does. That
made a 500-to-1000-term flat 'x := 1+1+...+1;' chain -- ordinary, if
unusual, input that compiles cleanly on main today -- get rejected with a
spurious "nested too deeply" the parser had no real stack-safety reason to
report; only Sema's own MaxExprDepth=1000 (Sema.h) should ever reject a
flat chain this short. 700 and 900 terms both sit inside that reverted
PR's false-rejection window (500-1000) while staying safely under Sema's
real limit, so both are checked here together: this file's whole point is
regression coverage for exactly the gap the adversarial review that killed
PR #553 found, not for the crash class itself (see the deeply-nested-*
tests elsewhere in this directory for that).
*)

